  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe probability of strong to severe thunderstorms has decreased\nthis evening with an exiting trough and drier conditions\nbuilding from the west. The main hazard through midnight will be\nlightning.\n\nMid- level troughing and cold advection lingers over the region\non Sunday, which may support isolated/scattered shower and \nthunderstorms development during the peak heating hours of \nSunday, mainly north of I-70. Although decreasing overall \nmoisture depth should keep a cap on coverage, one or two strong \nstorms may be possible given the cooling temperatures aloft, if\naround 1000 J/kg of surface-based CAPE can develop as hi-res \nmodels suggest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nShowers may linger north of I-80 and along the ridges (a cool-\nseason type of setup) into Monday morning with ongoing cool\nnorthwest flow over a warm Lake Erie.\n\nOtherwise, high pressure and height rises aloft point to a\nprolonged (relatively speaking, in relation to the previous\nmonth) dry period to start next week. High temperature does\nstart below seasonal averages on Monday before rising back near\naverage by mid week. Long range ensembles currently favor\nThursday as the next day with showers/thunderstorms as the next\nupper trough crosses.",
